# Educational Desktop App — Architecture Rules

These rules apply **only** to this Tanulós Program workspace. Follow them for every change.

## Stack (required)

- **Desktop:** Tauri v2 (Rust + WebView)
- **UI:** React 19 + TypeScript + Vite
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS — vibrant gradients, glassmorphism, `rounded-2xl` / `rounded-3xl`
- **Motion:** `motion/react` for micro-interactions and transitions
- **Effects:** `canvas-confetti` (and Rive later for mascot); keep rewards instant
- **Canvas / mini-games:** Pixi.js — keep game/canvas logic **separate** from React layout
- **Data:** Dexie.js (IndexedDB) + static JSON / module config — offline-first

## Directory layout

```text
src/
├── assets/
├── core/
│   ├── audio/      # SFX hooks
│   ├── db/         # Dexie schemas & helpers
│   ├── layout/     # Shell, nav, main container
│   └── store/      # Profile, score, streak
├── components/
│   ├── ui/         # Buttons, cards, motion wrappers
│   └── effects/    # Confetti, reward particles
├── modules/
│   ├── math/
│   ├── language/
│   └── registry.ts
├── types/module.ts
├── App.tsx
└── main.tsx
```

Never dump domain tasks into `components/` or `core/`. New subjects live under `src/modules/[name]/`.

## Module contract (`src/types/module.ts`)

Every module must implement `EducationalModule` and register in `modules/registry.ts`:

```typescript
export interface TaskProps<T = unknown> {
  config: T;
  onComplete: (score: number) => void;
  onFail: () => void;
}

export interface EducationalModule {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  icon: string;
  accentColor: string;
  tasks: {
    id: string;
    title: string;
    component: React.ComponentType<TaskProps>;
  }[];
}
```

## AI / implementation guidelines

1. **Strict modular isolation** — tasks stay inside `src/modules/[module-name]/tasks/`.
2. **Visual polishing** — rich Tailwind: gradients, glass panels, large touch targets, kid-friendly contrast.
3. **Animations first** — wrap state changes and screens in `motion` / `AnimatePresence`; aim for 60 FPS, zero-latency feedback.
4. **Performance** — avoid heavy re-renders; Pixi canvas logic stays outside React layout trees.
5. **Feedback loop** — correct answers: color change + confetti + sound (optional) + then `onComplete(score)`.
6. **Reuse existing UI** — use shared components from `components/ui` and `components/effects`; do not invent parallel button/toast/card systems.

## Sample task pattern

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";
import { motion } from "motion/react";
import confetti from "canvas-confetti";
import type { TaskProps } from "../../../types/module";

export const SimpleAdditionTask: React.FC<TaskProps> = ({ onComplete }) => {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<number | null>(null);
  const correctAnswer = 8;
  const options = [5, 8, 12, 6];

  const handleSelect = (option: number) => {
    setSelected(option);
    if (option === correctAnswer) {
      confetti({ particleCount: 80, spread: 60, origin: { y: 0.7 } });
      setTimeout(() => onComplete(100), 1000);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center p-8 space-y-6">
      <motion.h2
        initial={{ scale: 0.8, opacity: 0 }}
        animate={{ scale: 1, opacity: 1 }}
        className="text-4xl font-extrabold text-white"
      >
        Mennyi 5 + 3?
      </motion.h2>
      <div className="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        {options.map((option) => (
          <motion.button
            key={option}
            whileHover={{ scale: 1.05 }}
            whileTap={{ scale: 0.95 }}
            onClick={() => handleSelect(option)}
            className={`px-8 py-6 rounded-2xl text-2xl font-bold shadow-lg ${
              selected === option
                ? option === correctAnswer
                  ? "bg-green-500 text-white"
                  : "bg-red-500 text-white"
                : "bg-indigo-600 hover:bg-indigo-500 text-white"
            }`}
          >
            {option}
          </motion.button>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};
```

## UX defaults for this app

- Instant feedback (color + confetti + motion)
- Offline-first progression via Dexie
- Kid-friendly shell: large type, rounded-2xl/3xl, glass panels, vivid gradients
- Flow: home → module → task → reward → back
